Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

%labels
    Maintainer sdweave2@ncsu.edu
    Source https://github.com/ZhaoXM-Lab/VirRep
    Version git-7c7b4b8

%help
    VirRep — a hybrid language representation learning framework (context-aware
    + evolution-aware encoders) for identifying viral sequences in human gut
    metagenomes.

    This container was built from ZhaoXM-Lab/VirRep @ commit 7c7b4b8e43054a
    6d4da0b7a7d893c4f81441862e (no tagged release exists upstream).

    GPU: VirRep is torch-based and GPU-capable. This container ships a
    pip-installed PyTorch wheel built against CUDA 11.8 (matching the CUDA
    version upstream's own README documents: `pytorch-cuda=11.8`), with its
    own bundled CUDA 11.8 runtime — it does NOT require an nvidia/cuda base
    image. To actually use a GPU at runtime you MUST invoke it with:

        apptainer run --nv VirRep-git-7c7b4b8.sif [options]

    Without --nv (or on a login/CPU node) it runs correctly but on CPU only.

    Usage:
        apptainer run --nv VirRep-git-7c7b4b8.sif -i <input.fasta> -o <outdir> [options]

    Key options (see `apptainer run VirRep-git-7c7b4b8.sif -h` for the full list):
        -i INPUT_FILE, --input-file INPUT_FILE
                              Input fasta file of sequences to score.
        -o OUTPUT_DIR, --output-dir OUTPUT_DIR
                              Output directory for predictions.
        --label LABEL         Prefix to append to output filenames.
        -m MODEL, --model MODEL
                              Model path. Default 'model/VirRep.pth' is resolved
                              relative to the current working directory — the
                              image ships the pretrained model at
                              /opt/VirRep/src/model/VirRep.pth, so pass
                              -m /opt/VirRep/src/model/VirRep.pth explicitly
                              unless you cd into /opt/VirRep/src first.
        --use-amp             Use automatic mixed precision (GPU only).
        --conservative         Apply conservative high-confidence-only settings.
        -l MINLEN, --minlen MINLEN
                              Minimum sequence length to predict (>= 1000, default 1000).
        --provirus-off         Skip the iterative segment extension procedure.
        -w WORKERS             Number of worker processes.

    Example:
        apptainer run --nv VirRep-git-7c7b4b8.sif \
            -i test/test.fasta -o vr_out -w 2 --use-amp \
            -m /opt/VirRep/src/model/VirRep.pth \
            --min-score 1000-5000:0.9,5001-10000:0.8,10001-Inf:0.7

%post -c /bin/bash
    set -e
    # Header is exactly '%post -c /bin/bash' (Apptainer's %post ignores any
    # shebang line in the body; without -c /bin/bash it always runs under
    # dash, which has no 'set -o pipefail'). 'set -e' first so a failed
    # install step aborts the build instead of silently continuing with a
    # broken image (CLAUDE.md lesson 6).
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    # Hazel bind-mount points
    mkdir -p /rs1 /share /home /usr/local/usrapps

    # System packages
    apt-get update -qq && \
        apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
            python3 \
            python3-pip \
            git \
            ca-certificates && \
        apt-get clean && \
        rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # VirRep ships no setup.py/pyproject.toml/requirements.txt (Pattern 3) —
    # dependency list below is the AUTHORITATIVE set discovered by parsing
    # real `import`/`from` statements in the repo's .py files, not guessed
    # from the README. 'Bio' is the import name for the PyPI package
    # 'biopython'. torch is installed separately below with an explicit
    # CUDA pin.
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        biopython \
        numpy \
        packaging \
        pandas \
        tqdm

    # PREVIOUS BUILD FAILURE: this earlier version of the .def did a plain
    # 'pip3 install torch' with no index-url. On a bare 'pip install torch',
    # pip resolves the newest default wheel, which as of this generation is
    # built against CUDA 13 and pulls in an enormous, brand-new set of
    # nvidia-*-cu13 runtime packages (cublas 423MB, cudnn ~600MB+, cusolver
    # 200MB, cufft 214MB, cusparse 146MB, plus cu13-only additions that
    # don't even exist in older installs — nvidia-nvshmem-cu13,
    # nvidia-cusparselt-cu13, nvidia-cufile — on top of the usual curand,
    # nvjitlink, nvtx, nccl, cuda-nvrtc/runtime/cupti packages). %post and
    # %test both completed successfully against that install — the failure
    # was 'apptainer' getting SIGKILL'd (OOM) while running mksquashfs to
    # package the resulting multi-gigabyte rootfs into the final .sif, i.e.
    # the build host ran out of memory compressing an image that large, not
    # a content/logic bug in %post.
    #
    # FIX: pin the CUDA runtime torch is built against to 11.8 instead of
    # letting pip pick the newest default. This is not an invented pin —
    # it is the exact CUDA version the README itself specifies in its own
    # example install command ('conda install pytorch torchvision
    # torchaudio pytorch-cuda=11.8 -c pytorch -c nvidia'); pip's equivalent
    # mechanism for selecting a CUDA build is the '--index-url' wheel
    # index rather than a conda package pin. This both (a) matches
    # upstream's own documented CUDA target instead of guessing, and (b)
    # meaningfully shrinks the installed footprint versus the newest cu13
    # stack, which is the direct fix for the mksquashfs OOM above. As with
    # the plain-pip case, this wheel bundles its own CUDA 11.8 runtime — no
    # nvidia/cuda base image or CUDA_OVERRIDE is needed (that fix is only
    # required for Pattern 4/bioconda solves, see CLAUDE.md lessons 11-12).
    # PyPI's cu118 wheel index still requires a GPU with a driver new
    # enough to run CUDA 11.8 workloads and falls back to CPU automatically
    # when no GPU is visible (e.g. during this build, and on a login node).
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir torch \
        --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118

    # Fetch source and pin to the exact commit (no tagged release exists
    # upstream as of generation time). A prior version of this .def did a
    # full 'git clone' + 'git checkout <sha>', which pulls the ENTIRE repo
    # history (every revision of every file, including any earlier copies
    # of the shipped pretrained model checkpoint) into the image on top of
    # the working tree we actually need. Fetch only the single pinned
    # commit instead — GitHub supports fetching an arbitrary commit SHA
    # directly over its smart-HTTP protocol — matching template.def's own
    # Pattern-3 guidance to use a bounded '--depth 1' clone (a plain
    # '--branch <tag>' shallow clone isn't usable here since upstream has
    # no tag, only a bare SHA). Drop .git entirely once checked out; none
    # of it is needed at runtime.
    mkdir -p /opt/VirRep
    cd /opt/VirRep
    git init -q
    git remote add origin https://github.com/ZhaoXM-Lab/VirRep.git
    git fetch --depth 1 origin 7c7b4b8e43054a6d4da0b7a7d893c4f81441862e
    git checkout -q FETCH_HEAD
    rm -rf /opt/VirRep/.git

    # No console-script entry point ships with this repo (flat script under
    # src/) — create a bare-name wrapper so %runscript/%test and
    # container-mod's Programs parsing get a single resolvable executable.
    cat > /usr/local/bin/virrep << 'INNEREOF'
#!/bin/bash
exec python3 /opt/VirRep/src/VirRep.py "$@"
INNEREOF
    chmod +x /usr/local/bin/virrep

%environment
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    export PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

%runscript
    exec virrep "$@"

%test
    # VirRep.py uses Python's argparse; -h/--help is argparse's own built-in
    # help action (parser.exit(), exit code 0) — the script's one custom
    # sys.exit() path only fires when NO arguments at all are given, not on
    # -h, so --help's normal exit-0 behavior is unaffected here (verified
    # against src/VirRep.py; CLAUDE.md lesson 8 caveat does not apply).
    virrep --help
